    "reading_rules": {
      "fcr": "Dry feed supplied divided by net stage biomass gain (outgoing live biomass minus incoming live biomass). Economic FCR includes feed lost and mortality effects; do not add a second mortality feed multiplier. This is not a claim of constant FCR as survival changes.",
      "composition": "Use the same assumed wet whole-fish N and P content at entry and exit. Nonretained material includes uneaten feed, mortalities, dissolved excretion and solids; it is not all water discharge.",
      "oxygen": "Separate instantaneous standing-stock calculation. Fish respiration excludes nitrification and other biological demand. The latter excludes fish and nitrifiers. Basal nitrogen excretion and delayed decomposition are unresolved; feed-proportional terms do not model fasting or system upsets. The capacity factor is a design allowance, not a proven peak multiplier. Nitrification coefficients are oxidation-only stoichiometry from E10; other coefficients are assumptions.",
      "hatchery": "Batch yield is a planning assumption motivated by E08, not a validated count at the assumed cycle time or survival. Nominal water inventory is a separate hydraulic unit, not the volume of a 1000-glass-eel tank. Salt calculation assumes density 1000 kg/m3, tracks liquid losses, and excludes evaporation losses of salt.",
      "freshwater": "The 0.14 g/kg case is a physical inventory comparison only. No successful egg or larval performance is assigned to it; the actual calcium-bicarbonate ion recipe and magnesium floor remain unresolved.",
      "scope": "Feed and nutrient totals start at glass-eel entry. Broodstock, egg and larval feed, their wastes/oxygen/energy, and processing energy remain unquantified, not zero. Cooked fish excludes sauce, rice and coproduct credits. No annual yield per area or profitability is estimated."
    }
